 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Cache Replacement (2Q)
+
+The cache uses two queues over sheet entries:
+
+- **A1 (recency queue)** — a FIFO queue holding sheets admitted after their first completion. It absorbs one-pass traffic such as a long sequential screening: most of those sheets are viewed once and never promoted, so scanning evicts the scan's own entries rather than the comparison working set.
+- **A2 (reused queue)** — an LRU queue holding sheets that survived a second access. Repeatedly revisited comparison sheets live here and are evicted last.
+
+Admission and movement:
+
+- A newly admitted sheet enters A1 at the MRU end.
+- A hit on an A1 entry promotes it to A2's MRU end (removing it from A1).
+- A hit on an A2 entry refreshes that entry's position to A2's MRU end.
+
+Eviction, checked before each admission so the 12 GiB byte bound is respected:
+
+- A1 is bounded to one quarter of the cache's entry capacity (a ~2 MiB typical sheet makes entries near-uniform, so a count bound tracks the byte bound). When A1 is over its bound, evict A1's LRU entry — such an entry has had no second access and is evicted entirely.
+- Otherwise evict A2's LRU entry.
+
+The A1/A2 split ratio is tunable; one quarter is the starting value and may be adjusted after observing pilot screenings.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (A1 recency FIFO + A2 reused LRU) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Long screenings must not evict the comparison-sheet working set. LRU fails under sequential scans; aged frequency adds counter and aging bookkeeping and retains yesterday's favorites after a project switch; 2Q isolates one-pass traffic in A1 at the cost of two queues and a tunable split, all private to this worker. |
+| A1 sizing basis | Entry count bound (one quarter of entries) | Byte-based A1 budget | Sheets are near-uniform (~2 MiB), so a count bound approximates the byte bound with cheaper bookkeeping. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy is 2Q — chosen to protect reused comparison sheets during long screenings; the extra two-queue bookkeeping is accepted and stays private to the preview worker.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Tuning the A1/A2 split ratio (default one quarter) against pilot workload observations.
+2. Behavior when a promoted A2 entry is evicted and re-requested — it re-enters through A1 like any new admission; confirm this matches expectations in pilot data.
+
